## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates end-to-end diagnosis and mitigation of TLS handshake tail latency in multi-tenant Linux environments under production-like load conditions.

## Key Concepts
- TLS performance optimization, session resumption, and CPU pinning
- Hardware crypto offload and the kernel crypto API
- eBPF observability with per-tenant accounting
- Canary rollouts, staged rollouts, and rollback procedures

## Code Example
```bash
# Trace TLS handshake events with BPF (conceptual)
bpftrace -e 'tracepoint:tls:handshake {
  @tenant_id = comm;
  @latency = hist(nsecs - args->start_time);
}'
```

## Why This Is Asked

This situation tests ability to design robust multi-tenant resource governance with BPF maps, a common source of memory leaks in real-world deployments.

## Key Concepts

- BPF map accounting per tenant
- Map lifecycle and eviction strategies
- Namespace/isolation boundaries
- Observability with bpftool, perf, pidstat
- Canary rollouts and rollback plans

## Code Example

```c
// Pseudo: enforce per-tenant map limit
struct tenant {
  __u32 id;
  __u64 limit; // bytes
  __u64 used;
};
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle bursts exceeding quotas without impacting others?
- What metrics and alerts would you implement?
